  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES RETURN:\n\nAfter a drier and quiet day yesterday and today (Monday),\nmoisture will make its return as a deeper low pressure trough\nworks into the Great Basin and high pressure sits over Texas,\nallowing southerly flow to bring up sub-tropical moisture once\nagain. Showers will begin increasing across eastern Utah and the\nFour Corners as the trough approaches from the west. Showers and\nstorms will increase across western Colorado Tuesday afternoon\nwith scattered storms. A shortwave also moves across northeast\nUtah into southwest Wyoming with a robust jet streak and our CWA\nin the right entrance region. So storms that do form Tuesday\ncould be strong given the better shear environment with more\nlocalized heavy rainfall possible. \n\nPWATs increase to 180 to 200 percent of normal across SW \nColorado by Tuesday evening with the deepest moisture Wednesday \nand Thursday focused more across SW Colorado. Models seem to \nindicate a robust shortwave moving through the area overnight \nWednesday with a low circulation focused across southeast San \nJuans and southern valleys, but the SW San Juans and adjacent \nsouthern valleys appear to have the best potential for a few \ninches of precipitation and best forcing. The highest QPF for \nthis round is focusing around the southern San Juans and \nsouthern valleys. In fact, there are indications some spots \ncould get several inches, which increases the potential for \nflooding. Rainfall is possible in the central and northern \nmountains, but in general the amounts as lower. In coordination\nwith PUB and ABQ, went ahead and issued a Flood Watch for\nWednesday afternoon through Thursday night for the SW San Juans\n(COZ019) and San Juan River Basin (COZ023) where QPF amounts are\nhigher, forcing is better and confidence is higher. This area\nhas also seen plenty of rainfall over the last week with the\nmost recent stretch of moisture so even with two days of dry\nweather, the ground remains saturated. \n\nUncertainty increases on whether the moisture gets pushed out \nof the area or leaves enough to support showers on Friday but \nsome models are indicating another shortwave trough moving \nthrough on Friday keeping the weather active. Either way it \nappears that moisture will not go far so it will not take much \nto return.",
